    "created_at": "2016-11-18T18:32:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-18T18:32:17Z",
    "body": "I'm a bit confused as to what the actual user-facing benefit of this proposal is. That is, what do users get in return for all the effort to standardize this, convince browsers to implement it, implement it, and then get as many web pages as possible to use it?\n\nThe most I can find is \"Therefore, the conforming UA should show user a warning if the input device is in upper or lower case lock.\" However, this doesn't seem to be a sufficient benefit. The only case in which a user is not immediately aware of the case they are locked into is password fields, and that's better addressed by just asking browsers to warn users when they have case-lock on and are typing into a password field. Such a proposal doesn't require spec changes or for web pages to update to sprinkle this attribute everywhere.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/261605820/reactions",
